    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay explores the connective threads across the multimedial works of Zhang Kechun, Chen Qiulin, and Jia Zhangke, focusing on their depictions of estranged landscapes and the resulting invocation of a gap between the idealized visions of the past and the camera’s indexical capturing of the material environment in the 21st century. By engaging with literature across the conceptual fields of ecocriticism, modernity, Chinese historical landscapes, Chinese environmental history, sociology, and cinema and media studies, the essay addresses two main questions: what geopolitical factors drive their artistic visions, and what does the revealed gap suggest about the ecocritical potential of landscape as an art form and idea in the age of the Anthropocene?\r\n\r\nThe analysis delves into the nuanced relationship between contemporary landscape representations and their historical contexts, avoiding simplistic arguments about loss and nostalgia by examining the sophisticated narrative strategies employed by these artists. The essay also draws parallels between the “new topographic” photographers in the West and the Chinese artists, highlighting how artists across cultural contexts forge new grounds of landscape representation by drawing on older traditions of the Picturesque while addressing the ethical demand of their cameras by focusing faithfully on a nature long altered. Through this comparative and interdisciplinary examination, the essay argues that Zhang, Chen, and Jia critically engage with historical landscape aesthetics to reflect on contemporary environmental and social issues, thereby opening up new angles for understanding the interplay between art and the environment, as well as between the media of painting, photography, and moving images.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
